---
import BlogLayout from "../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro";
import { posts } from "../../data/blog";
import { profile } from "../../data/profile";
const post = posts.find(
  (p) => p.slug === "why-swift-is-the-next-big-thing-for-iot",
)!;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BlogLayout
  title={`${post.title}, Lilly Seay`}
  description={post.description}
  image={post.cover}
>
  <article class="post prose-post">
    <header class="post-head wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Blog · My FOSDEM 2025 talk, Swift devroom, Brussels</p>
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
      <p class="post-meta">
        By {profile.name} · {post.date} · {post.readTime}
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="wrap post-body">
      <h2>Introduction</h2>
      <p>
        Hello. My name is Lilly Seay, and today I’ll be sharing why Swift is the
        next big thing for IoT.
      </p>
      <p>
        In 2016, I graduated with a degree in electrical engineering and started
        working full-time in hardware design and embedded systems. At that time,
        I started working on projects involving IoT, or Internet of Things,
        which connects everyday objects to the internet to make them smarter and
        more interactive. Take a simple example: a smart light that turns on
        when it detects motion and integrates seamlessly with the Home app.
      </p>
      <p>
        While this type of engineering was rewarding, I was eager to expand my
        skills into modern software development, specifically iOS. My days were
        spent writing low-level code in C for embedded systems. At night, I was
        coding in Swift, building my first iOS app.
      </p>
      <p>
        At the time, these two worlds—embedded systems and app development—felt
        miles apart. I remember coding in C and worrying that a single typo
        could crash my program or even damage my hardware. That constant caution
        made Swift’s modern features—like type safety, automatic memory
        management, and interoperability with C—even more exciting, hinting at
        its potential to bring these two worlds together.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now if we fast forward to 2024, Embedded Swift was announced, and
        suddenly, my two worlds in engineering didn’t just overlap—they
        intersected.
      </p>
      <p>
        Traditionally, developers relied on C/C++ — precise, powerful languages
        that offers full control over hardware. However, that precision comes
        with challenges, like manual memory management, complex error handling,
        and a steep learning curve.
      </p>
      <p>
        Embedded Swift, on the other hand, excels at bringing modern, safe, and
        scalable code to hardware development, and today, I’m going to show you
        how I used it to create a smart light with motion detection.
      </p>
      <p>
        I have a motion sensor that detects motion and sends a high signal to
        the ESP32C6. This signal updates the LED state to either ON or OFF, and
        the change reflects in the Home app.
      </p>
      <p>
        We’re starting with the Matter example in Swift. This project was
        featured in the video Go Small with Embedded Swift. Today, I’m only
        going over the additions I made for my motion sensor, but if you’re
        interested in the rest of the Matter &amp; LED implementation, go
        checkout the repo.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’m using Embedded Swift, an ESP32C6 board, a motion sensor, and the
        Matter protocol.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here's a quick close up of what my circuit is looking like for the
        motion sensor.
      </p>
      <h2>Demo</h2>
      <p>I have my motion sensor smart light here with me today</p>
      <figure class="video-figure reveal">
        <video
          controls
          playsinline
          preload="metadata"
          poster={`${base}/blog/swift-iot/motion-light-poster.jpg`}
        >
          <source
            src={`${base}/blog/swift-iot/motion-light-demo.mp4`}
            type="video/mp4"
          />
          Your browser does not support the video tag.
        </video>
      </figure>
      <p>
        I have my program running in VSCode and the log statements are showing
        up in the terminal.
      </p>
      <p>
        First, I added the GPIO driver for the motion sensor, interrupt headers,
        and a Matter C++ call that updates Home app inside the BridgingHeader.h,
        enabling C/C++ interoperability.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now we can move on to the core functionality of the sensor in
        MotionSensor class. In the initializer, I added two main functions:
        setupGPIO and setupTask.
      </p>
      <pre><code>final class MotionSensor &#123;
    fileprivate let pin: Int32
    private var motionDetectedCallback: (() -&gt; Void)?
    fileprivate var interruptQueue: OpaquePointer?

    init(pin: Int32, onMotionDetected: @escaping () -&gt; Void) &#123;
        self.pin = pin
        self.motionDetectedCallback = onMotionDetected

        if let error = setupGPIO() &#123;
            print("GPIO setup failed: \(error.description)")
            esp_restart() // Restart the ESP32 system
        &#125;

        if let error = setupTask() &#123;
            print("Task setup failed: \(error.description)")
            esp_restart() // Restart the ESP32 system
        &#125;
    &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
      <p>
        The setupGPIO function is responsible for configuring the GPIO pin to
        interface with the motion sensor, ensuring it can properly receive input
        signals whenever motion is detected.
      </p>
      <pre><code>private func setupGPIO() -&gt; GPIOError? &#123;
    print("Configuring GPIO pin \(pin)...")

    var config = gpio_config_t(
        pin_bit_mask: 1 &lt;&lt; UInt64(pin),
        mode: gpio_mode_t(GPIO_MODE_INPUT.rawValue),
        pull_up_en: gpio_pullup_t(GPIO_PULLUP_DISABLE.rawValue),
        pull_down_en: gpio_pulldown_t(GPIO_PULLDOWN_ENABLE.rawValue),
        intr_type: gpio_int_type_t(GPIO_INTR_POSEDGE.rawValue)
    )

    if gpio_config(&amp;config) != ESP_OK &#123;
        return GPIOError(code: .configurationFailed(pin: pin))
    &#125;
    if gpio_install_isr_service(0) != ESP_OK &#123;
        return GPIOError(code: .isrServiceInstallationFailed)
    &#125;
    if gpio_isr_handler_add(
        gpio_num_t(pin),
        globalGPIOISRHandler,
        UnsafeMutableRawPointer(Unmanaged.passUnretained(self).toOpaque())
    ) != ESP_OK &#123;
        return GPIOError(code: .isrHandlerAdditionFailed(pin: pin))
    &#125;
    return nil
&#125;</code></pre>
      <p>
        The second function, setupTask, establishes a thread-safe interrupt
        handler for the GPIO pin. This interrupt is triggered whenever the
        motion sensor detects activity, allowing the system to respond in
        real-time. When the interrupt fires, the LED state is updated, creating
        a seamless connection between sensor input and hardware output.
      </p>
      <pre><code>private func setupTask() -&gt; GPIOError? &#123;
    interruptQueue = xQueueGenericCreate(10, UBaseType_t(MemoryLayout&lt;Int32&gt;.size), UInt8(queueQUEUE_TYPE_BASE))
    guard let interruptQueue else &#123;
        return GPIOError(code: .interruptQueueCreationFailed)
    &#125;

    let result = xTaskCreate(&#123; arg in
        guard let arg = arg else &#123; return &#125;
        let instance = Unmanaged&lt;MotionSensor&gt;.fromOpaque(arg).takeUnretainedValue()
        instance.processInterrupts()
    &#125;, "InterruptTask", 2048, Unmanaged.passUnretained(self).toOpaque(), 1, nil)

    guard result == pdPASS else &#123;
        return GPIOError(code: .interruptTaskCreationFailed)
    &#125;
    return nil
&#125;

private func processInterrupts() &#123;
    var pin: Int32 = 0
    while true &#123;
        if xQueueReceive(interruptQueue, &amp;pin, portMAX_DELAY) == pdTRUE &#123;
            print("Motion detected on GPIO \(pin)!")
            motionDetectedCallback?()
        &#125;
    &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
      <p>
        One thing I appreciate about Swift is its structured approach to error
        handling If any part of this configuration fails—like setting up the pin
        or installing the interrupt service—the function throws a specific
        error. And here’s a great example for my GPIO pin
      </p>
      <p>
        I implemented a custom extension that leverages Swift’s robust
        error-handling features. The extension defines specific error cases that
        can be managed using Swift’s do-catch or if let blocks.
      </p>
      <pre><code>extension MotionSensor &#123;
    struct GPIOError &#123;
        enum Code &#123;
            case configurationFailed(pin: Int32)
            case isrServiceInstallationFailed
            case isrHandlerAdditionFailed(pin: Int32)
            case interruptQueueCreationFailed
            case interruptTaskCreationFailed
        &#125;

        let code: Code

        var description: String &#123;
            switch code &#123;
            case .configurationFailed(let pin):
                return "Failed to configure GPIO pin \(pin)."
            case .isrServiceInstallationFailed:
                return "Failed to install GPIO ISR service."
            case .isrHandlerAdditionFailed(let pin):
                return "Failed to add ISR handler for GPIO pin \(pin)."
            case .interruptQueueCreationFailed:
                return "Failed to create interrupt queue."
            case .interruptTaskCreationFailed:
                return "Failed to create interrupt task."
            &#125;
        &#125;
    &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
      <p>
        If any of my functions throw an error, I use an if let block to
        gracefully catch the issue and immediately restart the ESP device to
        recover from the error. This approach improves the reliability of the
        system by ensuring potential issues are handled effectively at runtime.
      </p>
      <p>
        After my sensor detects motion, I needed to sync the LED state with the
        Home app. Let’s head over to Matter.swift where I extended pre-existing
        Matter functionality to include a function that reports changes in the
        LED state triggered by either the motion sensor or the Home ap. Here we
        have reportLightState(). This function takes the current state of the
        LED and updates the Matter protocol.
      </p>
      <p>
        Next, in Main.swift, I created an instance of the MotionSensor class to
        handle motion detection, initializing it on GPIO pin 5. I decided to use
        a closure in Swift because it makes my motion sensor detection logic
        easy to read. When my interrupt fires, I’ve setup a callback to execute
        the code in this closure.
      </p>
      <p>
        I have a variable led.enabled that was already setup for the LED; it
        updates the state of the LED. I make sure to toggle this when my motion
        sensor is triggered so the LED will turn on or off. After that, I use my
        reportLightState function to update the Matter stack so the Home app
        will stay in sync with the LED’s current state.
      </p>
      <pre><code>// Add the motion sensor
let motionSensor = MotionSensor(pin: 5) &#123;
    // update LED state
    led.enabled.toggle()
    lightEndpoint.reportLightState(to: led.enabled)
&#125;</code></pre>
      <p>
        To wrap up, I set the board target, built the project, flashed it to the
        ESP32C6, and monitor the program. If we go back to the terminal, we can
        see logs for every LED state change.
      </p>
      <p>
        The result is a fully functional smart light that responds to motion and
        stays synced with Home app.
      </p>
      <h2>Conclusion</h2>
      <p>
        Embedded Swift has changed the way I do IoT Development. It prevents
        memory leaks with automatic memory management, avoids error-prone code
        with built-in safety features, and is easier for me to read. Its small
        memory footprint makes it ideal for IoT devices.
      </p>
      <p>
        But Swift’s potential doesn’t stop there. It connects every part of
        IoT—embedded systems, back-end infrastructure, and applications. This
        unified approach simplifies development, removes barriers for startups
        and indie developers, and enables the creation of efficient, scalable,
        and user-friendly products.
      </p>
      <p>And That is why Swift is the next big thing for IoT. Thank you.</p>

      <section class="post-outro reveal">
        <h2>Watch the talk</h2>
        <p>
          The recording is on the FOSDEM archive:
          <a
            href="https://archive.fosdem.org/2025/schedule/event/fosdem-2025-6148-why-swift-is-the-next-big-thing-for-iot/"
            >talk page</a
          > and
          <a
            href="https://video.fosdem.org/2025/k4401/fosdem-2025-6148-why-swift-is-the-next-big-thing-for-iot.mp4"
            >video</a
          >. The project starts from Apple's <a
            href="https://github.com/apple/swift-matter-examples"
            >swift-matter-examples</a
          > repo and the WWDC24 session
          <a href="https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2024/10197/"
            >Go small with Embedded Swift</a
          >. Building something that needs an app and a device to talk to each
          other? <a href={`mailto:${profile.email}`}>Send me a note</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BlogLayout>
